// action_rules.qbsk — a QBSK-language Action + Rules engine.
//
// An earlier release reopening the CDDA/Qud audit's
// "D2: Action/Rules" item (the roadmap §4.3, §5.2) — the item right
// after D1 (ECS Foundation, src/ecs/, still unwired: no real scene has measured a need
// for it, see the D1-D5 "NOT adopted" decision above). D2 does not have that problem:
// it is built here, from scratch, as an ordinary QBSK library — zero new natives, zero
// new syntax — per this project's "QBSK libraries first, natives last resort" principle.
// The only primitive it needs already exists: first-class functions (docs/language.md
// §5.3 — "map(l, fn)... first-class functions... a named function defined with func").
// A rule IS a QBSK function value, stored in a plain list, called through a variable.
//
// The model (Qud's Action/Rules, cited in full in the audit doc, gridbugs.org): actions
// are dicts waiting to happen; rules are functions that veto or allow them; an accepted
// action may enqueue follow-on actions (bump -> open -> reveal; exile -> flee -> ...).
// This library is the generic loop only — what an action MEANS, and what accepting it
// actually changes in the world, is entirely up to the rule functions the caller writes.
// That is deliberate, the same restraint earlier releases both used: a generic "apply these
// changes to any entity" primitive is not built until a second real caller needs one —
// examples/worldgen_test.qbsk (an earlier release's own closing evidence) is the first.
//
// Contract a rule function must follow:
//   func myRule(action, state, pending) -> dict
//     "action"  — the dict currently being processed (whatever shape the caller chose)
//     "state"   — whatever shared state the caller passed to process(); read or mutate
//                 through it directly (dicts/lists are by-reference, docs/language.md
//                 §5.3) — this library never touches it. A rule that closes over the
//                 caller's own top-level vars instead (proven safe: docs/language.md
//                 line ~535, "assignments to outer variables persist") can ignore this
//                 parameter — examples/worldgen_test.qbsk's rules do exactly that.
//     "pending" — the SAME list process() is draining; push(pending, followOnAction) to
//                 enqueue a follow-on, exactly like Qud's follow_on_accepted queue
//     returns {"accept": bool} — false from ANY rule drops the action for good. A rule
//     indifferent to an action's type should default to {"accept": true} (no opinion),
//     not reject — rejecting is a veto, and only the rule that owns that action type
//     should ever cast one.
//
// Note: the natural parameter name for the third argument would be "world", but that's
// a reserved scene-DSL keyword (src/parser/parser.ts's SCENE_DSL_TOKENS) — "state" is
// used instead, everywhere in this file and in every caller.

export func process(pending, rules, state)
    var committed = []
    var rejected = []
    var i = 0
    while i < len(pending)
        var action = pending[i]
        var accepted = true
        var j = 0
        while j < len(rules)
            var rule = rules[j]
            var verdict = rule(action, state, pending)
            // The header promises `{"accept": bool}` and nothing checked it, while
            // `not verdict["accept"]` fell back to truthiness -- so a rule returning the
            // STRING "no" COMMITTED the action. The rule said no and the engine did it.
            //
            // That is the worst shape a defect takes here, because the veto's whole
            // point is that one rule can stop an action and no other rule's opinion
            // overrides it. A type slip inverted it silently, and the action went
            // through looking exactly like one every rule had approved.
            if type(verdict["accept"]) != "bool"
                fail("rule " + str(j) + " answered accept as a " + type(verdict["accept"]) + ", and a verdict is a bool -- a non-bool decided by truthiness, so a rule could veto and be committed anyway")
            if not verdict["accept"]
                accepted = false
            j += 1
        if accepted
            committed = push(committed, action)
        else
            rejected = push(rejected, action)
        i += 1
    return {"committed": committed, "rejected": rejected}
